TENSOR PUZZLES
==============

  My solutions to Sasha Rush's Tensor Puzzles.

      https://github.com/srush/Tensor-Puzzles

  Tensor Puzzles are 21 puzzles. Each asks you to reimplement one
  function in the NumPy standard library without magic.


RULES
-----

  1. These puzzles are about broadcasting. Know this rule.

  2. Each puzzle needs to be solved in 1 line (<80 columns) of code.

  3. You are allowed @, arithmetic, comparison, shape, any indexing
     (e.g. a[:j], a[:, None], a[arange(10)]), and previous puzzle
     functions.

  4. You are not allowed anything else. No view, sum, take, squeeze,
     tensor.

  5. You can start with these two functions:

         def arange(i: int):
             "Use this function to replace a for-loop."
             return torch.tensor(range(i))

         def where(q, a, b):
             "Use this function to replace an if-statement."
             return (q * a) + (~q) * b


SOLUTIONS
---------

   1  ones           arange(i) ** 0
   2  sum            a[None,:] @ ones(a.shape[0])
   3  outer          a[:,None] @ b[None, :]
   4  diag           a[arange(a.shape[0]), arange(a.shape[0])]
   5  eye            1*( arange(j) == arange(j)[:, None] )
   6  triu           1*( arange(j) >= arange(j)[:, None] )
   7  cumsum         a @ triu(a.shape[0])
   8  diff           a - (arange(i) > 0) * a[arange(i) - 1]
   9  vstack         where(arange(2)[:, None] == 0, a, b)
  10  roll           a[(arange(i) + 1) % i]
  11  flip           a[i - arange(i) - 1]
  12  compress       ( (cumsum(g * 1) * g) -1 == arange(i)[:, None] )*1 @ v
  13  pad_to         ( arange(i) == arange(j)[:, None] )*1 @ a
  14  sequence_mask  values * (arange(values.shape[1]) < length[:, None])
  15  bincount       (a == arange(j)[:, None])*1 @ ones(a.shape[0])
  16  scatter_add    (link == arange(j)[:, None])*1 @ values
  17  flatten        a[arange(i * j) // j, arange(i * j) % j]
  18  linspace       arange(n) * (j - i) / max(1, n - 1) + i
  19  heaviside      (a == 0) * b + (a > 0)
  20  repeat         (arange(d) * 0)[:, None] + a
  21  bucketize      (v[:, None] >= boundaries)*1 @ ones(boundaries.shape[0])


BONUS
-----

  Below are extra puzzles, not part of the original 21. They use the
  same format: one standard library function, reimplemented from the
  same toolbox.

  The requirements are relaxed for these: some need more than one
  line to remain legible.

  22  argmax
      sum((cumsum(((a[:, None] < a) * 1 @ a ** 0 == 0) * 1) == 0) * 1)
  23  cdist          (a[:, None] - b) ** 2 @ ones(a.shape[1])
  24  argsort
      x = a[:, None] > a
      y = (a[:, None] == a) * (1 - triu(a.shape[0]))
      ((x + y) @ a ** 0 == arange(a.shape[0])[:, None])*1 @ arange(a.shape[0])
  25  repeat_interleave
      v[(cumsum(c) <= arange(sum(c))[:, None])*1 @ ones(c.shape[0])]
